How to Switch Supabase Accounts

Open `main.dart` and replace these two values with your new project's credentials:

dart
await Supabase.initialize(
  url: ',   // ← change this
  anonKey: ,                   // ← and this
);


Find your URL and anon key in: Supabase Dashboard → Settings → API


 File-by-File Breakdown


 -main.dart-
The starting point of the entire application.

This is the first file Flutter runs. It does three things in order:
1. Initializes Supabase with the project URL and anon key
2. Pre-loads the work types and damage spaces from Supabase (so dropdowns are ready before the user opens any form)
3. Launches the app and wraps everything with the theme provider and connectivity overlay

If you're switching Supabase accounts, this is the only file you need to change.


-session.dart-
Stores the currently logged-in user's data for the whole app session.

After a successful login, the user's ID, name, username, role, and permission flags are saved here as a global singleton (`AppSession.instance`). Any screen in the app can read from it without needing to pass data through constructors.

Key getters:
- `isAdmin` — true if role is `admin`
- `isSubAdmin` — true if role is `sub_admin`
- `canAccessAdmin` — true for full admins OR sub-admins with the panel permission
- `canEditReports` — true for full admins OR sub-admins with the reports permission
- `initial` — first letter of the user's name, used for the avatar in the sidebar

On sign-out, `clear()` is called to wipe all stored data.


`theme_provider.dart`
The design system token file — controls every color, shadow, and style in the app.

The `Tk` class holds all design tokens (colors, shadows, etc.) in both light and dark variations. Instead of hardcoding `Color(0xFF1D6FE8)` everywhere, every screen just writes `_tk.blue` and the right shade is automatically picked based on the current mode.

Key parts:
- `Tk` class — the token holder. Pass `Tk(true)` for dark mode, `Tk()` for light
- `ThemeNotifier` — a global `ChangeNotifier` that lets the dark mode toggle button update the whole app
- `TkProvider` — an `InheritedWidget` that carries `Tk` down the widget tree
- `context.tk` — a shortcut extension so any widget can grab the current theme in one line
- `workColor(code)` — returns the accent color for a given work type code (101=blue, 102=cyan, etc.)
- `diffColor(level)` — returns green/amber/red for Minor/Moderate/Major difficulty


`pending_users_notifier.dart`
A global counter that tracks how many user accounts are waiting for admin approval.

This is a `ValueNotifier<int>` shared across the app.
The sidebar Admin badge and the admin hub tile both listen to it and update automatically whenever the count changes — no manual refreshing needed.It is the notification counts.

- `refresh()` — queries the `users` table for rows with `status = 'pending'` and updates the value
- Called at app startup, when opening the Admin page, and after approving/rejecting a user

 `admin_data.dart`
manages the work types and damage spaces that appear in job order form dropdowns.

Instead of hardcoding the dropdown options in the code, we stored them in Supabase so admins can add, edit, or delete them without a code update. This file has two `ChangeNotifier` classes — one for each table.

- `WorkTypesNotifier` — reads/writes the `work_types` table
- `DamageSpacesNotifier` — reads/writes the `damage_spaces` table
- Both expose `fetch()`, `add()`, `update()`, and `remove()` methods
- `initAdminData()` — called in `main.dart` at startup to load both lists in parallel
- `workTypesNotifier` and `damageSpacesNotifier`are global singletons imported wherever dropdowns are needed

 `notification_helper.dart`
Writes notification rows to Supabase whenever a job is assigned or marked accomplished

The mobile app reads from the `team_notifications` table to show the field team what's new. This helper is a static utility class — no instantiation needed, just call the methods directly.

- `notifyJobAssigned(...)` — fires when an office user assigns a team to a job. Inserts a row with `type: 'new_job'`
- `notifyJobAccomplished(...)` — fires when a job is marked done. Inserts a row with `type: 'accomplished'`
- Errors are caught silently — a failed notification should never interrupt the main save operation



 `ds.dart`
The shared widget library — reusable UI components used across every screen.
This is the "building block" file. Almost every screen imports this to get standard widgets that stay consistent with the design system.


 `DsHeader` - The top bar with icon, title, subtitle, and optional action buttons — used on every main screen
 `DsCard` - A themed surface card with border and shadow
 `DsLabel` - A small uppercase section label (e.g. "USER MANAGEMENT")
 `DsTag` - A color-coded pill/badge (e.g. "Pending", "Done", "Leader")
 `DsSearchBar` - A themed search field with a clear button
 `DsLoading` - A centered loading spinner shown while data is fetching
 `DsEmpty` - A centered empty state with an icon and message
 `dsInputOf(context, label)` - Returns an `InputDecoration` that matches the current theme — used on all text fields

 `connectivity_wrapper.dart`
Shows a full-screen "No Internet" overlay whenever the device loses connection.**

Every 4 seconds, it does a real DNS lookup to `google.com` to check if there's an actual working internet connection (not just WiFi with no internet). If the check fails, it slides up a dark overlay that blocks all interaction until connectivity is restored.

- Uses `InternetAddress.lookup()` with a 5-second timeout for the real check
- Has a "Try Again" button for manual retry
- Wraps the entire app inside `MaterialApp`'s `builder` so it covers every screen

 `login_screen.dart`
The login page and the sign-up page. First thing users see when they open the app.

The login screen queries the `users` table directly — no Supabase Auth, just a username + password match. It checks the account's `status` field and blocks login if the account is `pending`, `rejected`, or `blocked`.

On successful login:
1. The user's data is saved to `AppSession.instance`
2. The app navigates to `MainScreen` with a fade transition

The sign-up screen (also in this file) creates a new row in the `users` table with `status: 'pending'`. The account won't work until an admin approves it.

The animated background (floating glowing blobs) is handled by `_AnimatedBg` using an `AnimationController` that repeats every 8 seconds.

Also contains `SignUpScreen`, `_AuthLabel`, `_AuthField`, `_AuthButton`, `_AuthErrorBanner`, `_AuthSuccessBanner`, and `_LeftPanel` (the branding panel on the left side of the card).


 `signup_screen.dart`
Just re-exports `SignUpScreen` from `login_screen.dart`.**

Since `SignUpScreen` is defined inside `login_screen.dart` (to share auth widget code), this file is a thin re-export so other parts of the app can import it cleanly without importing the whole login screen file.


 `main_screen.dart`
The main shell of the app after login — contains the sidebar navigation and all screens.

This is what users see after logging in. It's a persistent layout with a collapsible sidebar on the left and the active screen taking up the rest of the space.

Sidebar items:

| Index | Screen | Who Sees It |
|-------|--------|-------------|
| 0 | Dashboard | Everyone |
| 1 | Complaints (Job Order) | Everyone |
| 2 | Job Orders (Team Jobs) | Everyone |
| 3 | Skeletal Jobs | Everyone |
| 4 | Team Status | Everyone |
| 5 | Accomplished | Everyone |
| 6 | Monthly Report | Everyone |
| 7 | Overall Report | Everyone |
| 8 | Relocation Report | Everyone |
| 9 | Settings | Everyone |
| 10 | Admin | Only `canAccessAdmin` users |

Key features:
- The sidebar can be fold to just icons to save space in the sidebar.
- The -Reports- section is a collapsible group inside the sidebar
- The -Admin- tile shows a red badge with the count of pending user approvals (live from `pendingUsersNotifier`)
- The -dark mode toggle- in the dashboard updates `themeNotifier`, which triggers a rebuild here
- The -sign-out dialog- clears `AppSession` and navigates back to `LoginScreen`


`dashboard_screen.dart`
The analytics dashboard — shows charts and stats about all accomplished jobs.
This is the first screen users see after logging in. It pulls data from both `job_orders` and `skeletal_job` tables and presents it as interactive charts.

Charts included:
- Stat cards — total Pending, Accomplished, and Completion Rate %
- Job Order Accomplishment — a bar chart showing jobs completed over time (switch between Days / Weeks / Months / Year)
- Work Type Distribution — a pie chart showing the breakdown of work types (excludes Restoration code 203 which gets its own chart)
- Damaged Space Distribution — a pie chart for surface types (Concrete, Sidewalk, Earth, etc.)
- Restoration Surface Breakdown — a separate pie chart just for Restoration (code 203) jobs
- Work + Surface Combinations — a combo bar chart of the top 10 most common job-surface pairings
- Surface Type by Address — a scrollable bar chart showing which barangays have the most jobs per surface type
- Address Breakdown — four separate bar charts, one per work type group, showing job counts per barangay

Filtering: Users can filter all charts by a specific month/year or view overall (all-time) data using the filter buttons at the top.

Dark mode toggle lives here as the premium animated sliding switch.


 `job_order_screen.dart
The Complaints screen — where new complaints are entered and teams are assigned.

This screen has a split two-column layout:
- Left panel — Unassigned complaints waiting for a team
- Right panel — Active job orders that already have a team assigned

Workflow:
1. A complaint comes in → office staff clicks "New Complaint" → fills out the form (work type, difficulty, damage space, barangay) → saves it
2. The complaint appears in the left panel
3. Staff clicks "Assign" on a complaint → picks a team and shift time → the complaint moves to the right panel as an official job order

Key features:
- Auto-generates the J.O. number in `YYYY-NNNNN` format (e.g. `2026-00045`) by checking the highest existing sequence
- Stats bar shows total unassigned, active J.O. count, top work type, and difficulty breakdown
- Newly created cards flash briefly with a blue glow so you can spot them
- Both panels have independent search bars

`team_jobs_screen.dart`
The Job Orders screen — where assigned job orders can be edited and marked as accomplished.

Shows all pending job orders that already have a team assigned. Unlike the Complaints screen, this one is for managing and completing existing orders.

Key features:
- Click any card to open the full edit dialog — change the team, shift, work type, difficulty, damage space, address, remarks, and zone
- "Mark as Accomplished" button inside the dialog — prompts for confirmation, then sets status to `accomplished`, records who did it (`accomplished_by_user_id`), and fires a notification to the team's mobile app via `NotificationHelper.notifyJobAccomplished()`
- Search works across J.O. number, team name, work type, shift, and address


 `accomplished_screen.dart`
Shows all completed job orders and skeletal jobs.

Combines data from both `job_orders` (status = `accomplished`) and `skeletal_job` (status = `completed`) into one unified list, sorted by completion date.

Key features:
- Month/Year filter — pick a specific month or view all-time. Has quick tabs for "This Month" and "Overall"
- Click any card to open a full detail view showing job info, team members with avatars, timeline, and activity log (who created it, who edited it, who accomplished it)
- The detail view also has an Edit button that opens the full edit dialog
- Editing is done directly in `skeletal_job` or `job_orders` depending on the type

