`delete_jobs_page.dart`
Lets admins select multiple jobs to move to the Trash Bin.

Shows all pending and accomplished job orders plus skeletal jobs in a selectable list.
Admins tap to select, then hit "Move to Trash" to soft-delete them (sets `status = 'deleted'` — they're not gone yet).
Jobs in the trash can be restored or permanently deleted from the Trash Bin page.

 `delete_teams_page.dart`
Lets admins permanently delete teams.
Similar multi-select UI to the delete jobs page, but for the `teams` table. This is a -permanent delete- with no trash bin — the team row is gone immediately. A confirmation dialog appears before anything is deleted.

`trash_bin_page.dart
Shows all soft-deleted jobs and lets admins restore or permanently delete them.
Combines trashed regular job orders and trashed skeletal jobs into one list.

Actions:
- Restore — sets the status back. Regular jobs go back to `pending`. Skeletal jobs go back to `completed` if they were done before deletion, or `pending` if they weren't
- Delete Forever — permanently removes the row from the database. Cannot be undone

`monthly_report_screen.dart`
Generates the Monthly Accomplished Jobs Report as a printable/downloadable PDF.

Pulls all accomplished job orders for the selected month/year and auto-fills a spreadsheet-like table with: number, address, foreman name, date reported, date accomplished, work type, remarks, and zone.

Key features:
- Admins and sub-admins with edit permission can click any cell to edit the text directly
- Formatting toolbar lets you bold, italic, underline, justify, or change font size per cell
- "Export PDF" button generates a proper legal-size landscape PDF and opens the share/download dialog
- "Add Row" button lets you manually add extra rows that aren't in the database
- Month/year picker is a dropdown in the header

 `overall_report_screen.dart`
Generates the Monthly Accomplishment Report (summary format) as a PDF.**

This is a different, more formal document than the monthly report. It's a structured table showing cumulative numbers across three columns: Previous Month, This Month, and Total to Date.

Items tracked:
1. Complaints Received
2. Complaints Acted Upon
3. Service Connection Repaired
4. Distribution Line Repaired
5. Distribution Line Replaced (Length)
6. Restoration
7. Water Meter Relocated (split into two sub-items)

- "This Month" numbers come live from Supabase
- "Previous Month" numbers are editable fields (manually entered or auto-suggested from last month's data)
- Signatories at the bottom are also editable text fields
- Edit Mode toggle shows/hides the editing UI
- "Print / Download" generates a short-paper (8.5×11) portrait PDF


`relocation_report_screen.dart`
Generates the Water Meter Relocation Report as a PDF.
Shows all accomplished job orders with work type code `301` (Relocation) for the selected month. Displays them in a table with: date accomplished, account number, concessionaire name, address, and remarks.

- All cells are editable for admins with edit permission
- "Add Row" and "Delete Last" buttons for manual entries
- "Export PDF" generates a legal-size landscape PDF

 `settings_screen.dart`
The user settings page — profile editing, dark mode, app info, and sign-out.

Sections:
- Profile — shows the logged-in user's name and username. "Edit Profile" opens a dialog to change the full name and/or password
- Appearance — a premium animated light/dark mode toggle with a live preview of both themes
- About — shows project credits (initiative, project lead, developers, beta testers) in a popup dialog
- Account — a sign-out button that clears the session and returns to the login screen

The dark mode toggle is an animated sliding switch that smoothly transitions colors and the thumb icon between sun and moon.


`connectivity_wrapper.dart`
Full-screen "No Internet" overlay that blocks the app when offline.

Wraps the entire app. Every 4 seconds it checks for real internet access using `InternetAddress.lookup('google.com')`. If the check fails, a dark overlay slides up from the bottom blocking all interaction. When connectivity is restored, the overlay automatically slides back down.
The overlay has a pulsing wifi-off icon, a "Try Again" manual retry button, and a small indicator showing that it's checking every 4 seconds.


 How Everything Connects

main.dart
  └── Initializes Supabase + loads admin data
  └── Wraps app with TkProvider + ConnectivityWrapper
  └── Starts at LoginScreen

LoginScreen
  └── Authenticates against 'users' table
  └── Saves data to AppSession
  └── Navigates to MainScreen

MainScreen
  └── Sidebar with 11 navigation items
  └── Shows the selected screen in the main area
  └── Listens to pendingUsersNotifier for the Admin badge
  └── Listens to themeNotifier for dark mode

Each Screen
  └── Reads AppSession for user info and permissions
  └── Uses context.tk for all colors and styles
  └── Queries Supabase directly for its own data
  └── Uses DsHeader, DsLoading, DsEmpty from ds.dart
  └── Uses workTypesNotifier / damageSpacesNotifier for dropdowns

Key Design Decisions

**No complex state management package** — I used Flutter's built-in `ChangeNotifier` and `ValueNotifier`. The app is simple enough that Redux or Riverpod would be overkill.

**Supabase queries are done directly in each screen** — No repository layer. Each screen fetches its own data. This keeps things simple and easy to trace.

**Admin sub-pages open inline** — Instead of using `Navigator.push` for admin sub-pages, they swap content in place. This keeps the sidebar visible at all times so navigation doesn't feel broken.

**Soft delete before permanent delete** — Jobs are never deleted immediately. They go to a Trash Bin first (status = 'deleted') and only get permanently removed when the admin explicitly chooses "Delete Forever" from the Trash Bin.

**Two separate user systems** — Office staff use the `users` table with plain password matching. Field teams use the `profiles` table with bcrypt-hashed passwords. This keeps the two systems completely independent.


